Give the base environment room, and refuse to write past it.

nix::EvalState allocates the base environment once, at a size that
src/libexpr/eval.cc fixes as a compile-time constant:

    static constexpr size_t BASE_ENV_SIZE = 128;

EvalState::addConstant and EvalState::addPrimOp then fill it with
`baseEnv.values[baseEnvDispl++] = v;` and test no bound at all. Nix 2.34
publishes 119 names under `builtins`, and each one takes a slot, so a stock
evaluator fits with 9 slots to spare.

nanopynix exposes primop registration (`nanopynix.register_primop`, which
reaches nix::RegisterPrimOp in nanopynix-bindings/src/nix_expr.cpp), and
createBaseEnv walks that global registry with no limit. The
`_register_test_primops` fixture in tests/conftest.py registers 24 primops,
which gives 143 names against 128 slots. The evaluator then writes 120 bytes
past the end of the base environment while it constructs itself.

AddressSanitizer, on the nix_2_34-asan build of issue #47, names the write:

    ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow
    WRITE of size 8
        #0 nix::EvalState::addPrimOp(nix::PrimOp&&)               eval.cc:550
        #1 nix::EvalState::createBaseEnv(nix::EvalSettings const&) primops.cc:5427
        #2 nix::EvalState::EvalState(...)                         eval.cc:349
    0 bytes after 1032-byte region  <- allocEnv(BASE_ENV_SIZE)

**The collector hides this rather than preventing it.** EvalMemory::allocBytes
is GC_MALLOC in a normal build, and Boehm rounds a request up to a size class,
so the extra bytes land in the slack of the block and nothing reports them. A
build with `-Dgc=disabled` gets a plain calloc, which gives the exact size, so
glibc aborts with "corrupted size vs. prev_size". Both builds make the same
out-of-bounds write.

Why the size is a constant at all, which is the part worth reading:

- `Env` is a variable-length struct, `{ Env * up; Value * values[0]; }`. The
  parser compiles each variable reference into a (level, displacement) pair,
  and evaluation reads `env->values[displ]`. The array must be one contiguous
  block with a stable address, and `baseEnv` is a reference member, so an Env
  cannot grow after it exists.
- The allocation happens in the member-initialiser list of the constructor
  (eval.cc:308 and eval.cc:311), which runs before the constructor body.
  `createBaseEnv` is what knows how many names there are, and the body is what
  calls it. Nothing has counted at the moment of allocation, so the size is a
  constant for want of a count, and not for a property of the evaluator.
- The size costs nothing but memory. Lookup is an index into a flat array
  whatever the size is. `allocEnv` carries a hand-written fast path for
  `size == 1`, which is where the attention to cost went.
- Registration cannot happen during evaluation. RegisterPrimOp::primOps() is
  read exactly once, by createBaseEnv. `nanopynix.register_primop` changes
  that registry at run time, but only an evaluator built afterwards sees the
  change, which is why tests/conftest.py orders the fixtures. So the count is
  available at construction, and the correct fix upstream is to move the
  allocation into the constructor body and take the size from the registry.

**There are two containers of 128, and not one.** `createBaseEnv` also builds
the `builtins` attribute set with `buildBindings(128)`, and both `addConstant`
and `addPrimOp` push into it through a `const_cast`, which goes around the
capacity assert of `BindingsBuilder`. `Bindings::push_back` is
`attrs[numAttrs++] = attr;` and holds no capacity to test at all. Correcting
only the base environment moves the report from `EvalMemory::allocEnv` to
`Bindings::push_back`, and ASAN on the nix_2_34-asan build shows exactly that.
Both sizes go up together.

This patch does not restructure anything, because it keeps the change to what
a carried patch should be. It raises both sizes to 512, which gives room for a
consumer that registers primops and costs about 3 KB for each evaluator, and
it makes the two base environment writes test the bound, so the next consumer
to exceed the size gets an error it can read rather than a corrupt heap. The
`builtins` set gets no such check, because `Bindings` does not record its own
capacity.

BASE_ENV_SIZE is 128 in 2.31, in 2.34 and in 2.35, and it is neither a meson
option nor a setting, so no configuration avoids this. The context of all
three hunks is identical in those three versions, so one patch serves them
all, and only the line numbers move.

--- a/src/libexpr/eval.cc
+++ b/src/libexpr/eval.cc
@@ -225,7 +225,7 @@
     }
 }
 
-static constexpr size_t BASE_ENV_SIZE = 128;
+static constexpr size_t BASE_ENV_SIZE = 512;
 
 EvalMemory::EvalMemory()
 {
@@ -478,6 +478,10 @@
 
         /* Install value the base environment. */
         staticBaseEnv->vars.emplace_back(symbols.create(name), baseEnvDispl);
+        if (baseEnvDispl >= BASE_ENV_SIZE)
+            throw Error(
+                "the base environment is full: it holds %1% names, and a consumer registered more primops than fit",
+                BASE_ENV_SIZE);
         baseEnv.values[baseEnvDispl++] = v;
         const_cast<Bindings *>(getBuiltins().attrs())->push_back(Attr(symbols.create(name2), v));
     }
@@ -547,6 +551,10 @@
         internalPrimOps.emplace(primOp.name, v);
     else {
         staticBaseEnv->vars.emplace_back(envName, baseEnvDispl);
+        if (baseEnvDispl >= BASE_ENV_SIZE)
+            throw Error(
+                "the base environment is full: it holds %1% names, and a consumer registered more primops than fit",
+                BASE_ENV_SIZE);
         baseEnv.values[baseEnvDispl++] = v;
         const_cast<Bindings *>(getBuiltins().attrs())->push_back(Attr(symbols.create(primOp.name), v));
     }
--- a/src/libexpr/primops.cc
+++ b/src/libexpr/primops.cc
@@ -5148,7 +5148,7 @@
     Value v;
 
     /* `builtins' must be first! */
-    v.mkAttrs(buildBindings(128).finish());
+    v.mkAttrs(buildBindings(512).finish());
     addConstant(
         "builtins",
         v,
